package source

// Package source holds the contents of a file being compiled and maps byte
// offsets in it to line and column positions. It is a port of the Go
// compiler's package source, function by function, and of the Span type
// that compiler keeps in its package ast, which lives here in the port so
// that the lexer and diag can name it without the syntax tree.
//
// The loader creates a File from the bytes it read. The lexer records the
// offset at which each line starts as it scans, starting with 0, so a File
// can answer position and line queries only once lexing has run. Spans
// elsewhere in the compiler refer to a File by its FileID and to positions
// in it by byte offset; this package is what turns those offsets into the
// line:column a diagnostic shows.
//
// A program of several files reads them into a Set, which finds a File by
// its FileID. The port's FileID differs from Go's: Go's is the file's
// display name, a string, and the port's is the file's index in the Set
// that holds it, so that a Span, which every token and syntax node carries,
// stays a small copyable value with no Text in it. A Span's file name is
// read through the Set instead. A File is made with its FileID, before it
// is lexed, and the Set it is added to checks that the FileID is the index
// it gets, so the two cannot disagree.
//
// Go's Files interface, which both a Set and a lone File satisfy, is not
// ported: Tack has no interface values, and a File cannot be returned from
// a lookup without being copied, which a value owning lists cannot be.
// Rendering a diagnostic takes a Set, which a single file compiled on its
// own is put in too, as the Go loader does for it.
//
// It is a leaf package and depends on nothing else in the compiler.
